WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:06.130
Fans of Guardians of the Galaxy know one thing
for sure: Peter Quill is a pretty gnarly dude.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:10.520
With a name like Star-Lord, it's pretty much
a moral imperative to become a kick-butt space

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:11.520
adventurer.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.360
But is there more to Quill than meets the
eye?

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.539
Here's what comic fans know about Star-Lord
that you don’t.

00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:19.429
Star-Lord's nickname is ironic

00:00:19.429 --> 00:00:23.949
Every hero needs a code-name, even if it's
one you make up for yourself that nobody else

00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:26.060
uses or has even heard of.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:26.740
“Star-Lord.”

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.340
“Who?”

00:00:29.349 --> 00:00:33.910
In the comics, though, the name is an ironic
one, as Quill's dad is literally a star lord

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.250
named J'Son, ruler of Spartax.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:40.610
He wants Quill to follow in his footsteps,
but Quill nopes out of it, keeping the name

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:42.780
as a bit of a middle finger to dad.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.370
Star-Lord has 3 different origin stories

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:48.620
There are two routes you can go with superhero
origins.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:54.460
Either you can have one origin that you retell
ten billion times, like Batman and his perpetually

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:59.010
dying parents, or you can just have a whole
bunch of different, conflicting origins to

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:00.210
keep things interesting.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:04.060
That's the case with Star-Lord, who has had
three different backstories.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:07.460
Originally, he was an astronaut who ended
up becoming a spacecop.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.940
That was tweaked later, introducing the idea
that he joined the galactic pirates known

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.630
as the Ravagers before becoming Star-Lord.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:18.710
And then there's the movie version, which
swipes elements from both while giving him

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:21.690
a totally different dad in the form of Kurt
Russell.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:25.820
At least none of Star-Lord's origin stories
involve slow motion pearls.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:28.710
He wasn't originally a Guardian of the Galaxy

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:32.560
When Star-Lord first appeared, he had nothing
to do with the Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.909
In fact, that's the case for every single
one of the film's characters with the exception

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:37.909
of Yondu.

00:01:37.909 --> 00:01:42.330
He was one of the original Guardians of the
Galaxy when they first debuted way back in

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:43.330
1969.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:48.450
It wasn't until 2008 that Star-Lord ended
up putting together a new team of Guardians

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.650
alongside Gamora, Rocket, Groot, and Drax.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:54.580
“They call themselves The Guardians of the
Galaxy.”

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.060
“What a bunch of a-holes.”

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:59.619
Gamora and Peter aren't an item

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:04.760
In the movies, there's a clear will they - won't
they flirtation between Star-Lord and Gamora.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.119
But in the comics, you know, not so much.

00:02:07.119 --> 00:02:12.180
Gamora has historically been romantically
linked to Thanos's archenemy Adam Warlock,

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.510
though she also had a thing with the man called
Nova, Richard Ryder.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:20.709
Since he doesn't seem to exist yet in the
Marvel Cinematic Universe, that romance is

00:02:20.709 --> 00:02:24.630
a long shot on the big screen, but it has
a better chance of happening than Peter's

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.480
comic book romance with the X-Men's Kitty
Pryde.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.760
Since the movie rights to Kitty are tied up
at a different movie studio, their love is

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.099
tragically doomed.

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:35.230
Hey kids, comics!

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:39.489
One of the great things about comics is that
pretty much any random thing the writers can

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:42.400
possibly think of is bound to eventually happen.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.950
So while the adventures of the movie Star-Lord
have been pretty weird, the films are nothing

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:50.060
compared to the shenanigans he's gotten up
to in the comics.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:55.030
For instance, at one point he briefly became
king of the planet Spartax, while at another

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:59.620
point, he was trapped in an alternate dimension
called the Cancerverse where he was unable

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:00.620
to die.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:04.810
And in a twist the movie version of Peter
Quill would no doubt love, in one storyline

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.020
he became a professional lounge singer.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.599
Rock on, Star-Lord!

00:03:08.599 --> 00:03:10.500
Star-Lord dated his spaceship

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.940
Fans of the movie might be aware of the fact
that director James Gunn named their ship

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.450
The Milano after childhood crush Alyssa Milano.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:22.870
But in the comics, Star-Lord had a very different
ship who was a key part of his adventures

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.920
- because the ship was sentient.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:29.599
Known simply as Ship most of the time, Ship
was actually the imprisoned consciousness

00:03:29.599 --> 00:03:31.590
of a sentient star named Aurora.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:36.690
At one point, Ship managed to gain physical
form and totally made out with Star-Lord before

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:40.060
her body was destroyed and she just became
a starship again.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:42.260
That's… kind of creepy.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:43.940
There was another Star-Lord

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:48.610
It might be hard to imagine anyone but Chris
Pratt as Star-Lord, but during a brief stint

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:52.340
in the mid-'90s, a totally different character
took up the name.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:57.569
While Peter Quill was out of action, a psychic
space cop named Sinjin Quarrel found Ship

00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:01.680
and Star-Lord's gear and basically appropriated
everything for himself.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.129
Apparently when it comes to superhero codenames,
finders keepers, losers weepers.

00:04:06.129 --> 00:04:07.129
“Hey!

00:04:07.129 --> 00:04:08.440
If it isn’t Star-Prince.”

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:10.320
“It’s Star-Lord.”

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:11.580
“Oh, sorry.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:12.580
Lord.

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:15.110
I picked this guy up awhile back for petty
theft.

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:16.349
He’s got a codename!”

00:04:16.349 --> 00:04:17.479
Thanks for watching!

00:04:17.479 --> 00:04:20.570
